 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
 *   **Optical Depth Extremes:** If optical depth $\tau$ is infinitely large, transparency becomes 0. If $\tau$ is 0, transparency is 1 [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Scattering Neglect:** The model fails if scattering is significant, as it changes the direction and frequency of radiant energy [S9, S17, unofficial].
-*   **Aliasing:** Pre-classification and second-order integration can cause aliasing artifacts, especially when grid variables vary significantly between neighboring cells [S11].
-*   **Ordering:** Compositing requires strict ordering (back-to-front or front-to-back) because the equations are not commutative [S15, book].
-*   **Thickness Variation:** Constant transfer functions cause color variation based on ray thickness through an isosurface (darker/brighter) because rays accumulate different amounts of emission [S11].
+*   **Scattering Neglect:** The model fails if scattering is significant, as it changes the direction and frequency of radiant energy [S9, unofficial][S17].
+*   **Aliasing:** Pre-classification and second-order integration can cause aliasing artifacts, especially when grid variables vary significantly between neighboring cells [S11, unofficial].
+*   **Ordering:** Compositing requires strict ordering (back-to-front or front-to-back) because the equations are not commutative [S15].
