 ### Independent Source Model
-A variant where the source term $g(s)$ is defined independently of the extinction coefficient $\tau(s)$, allowing for transparent glowing gas (non-zero $g$ when $\tau$ is zero) [S13, unofficial]. Unlike the particle model where interpolated $g(s)$ is quadratic on a ray segment (if $f$ is linear), an independently defined and interpolated $g(s)$ is linear [S13, unofficial].
+A variant where the source term $g(s)$ is defined independently of the extinction coefficient $\tau(s)$, allowing for transparent glowing gas (non-zero $g$ when $\tau$ is zero) [S14, unofficial]. Unlike the particle model where interpolated $g(s)$ is quadratic on a ray segment (if $f$ is linear), an independently defined and interpolated $g(s)$ is linear [S14, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot R)$, where $N$ is the number of samples per ray and $R$ is the number of rays (pixels). Early ray termination can reduce effective $N$ for opaque regions [S10, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for storing the volume data, where $V$ is the number of voxels.
-*   **Performance:** Computationally tractable and suitable for interactive rendering, unlike full scattering models [S3, S10, S17, unofficial]. Performance is heavily dependent on the efficiency of volume traversal and interpolation [S8, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High realism for participating media like smoke and clouds; simple to implement; supports early ray termination; forms the basis for NeRFs [S3, S17, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Cannot model shadows cast by the volume onto itself or other objects (no in-scattering); inaccurate for media with significant scattering (e.g., fog with strong forward scattering, milk) [S9, S17, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Interactive visualization of medical data, scientific simulations, and neural radiance fields where scattering is negligible or approximated [S7, S13, S17, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot R)$, where $N$ is the number of samples per ray and $R$ is the number of rays (pixels) [S10, S19, unofficial]. Early ray termination can reduce effective $N$ for opaque regions [S10, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for storing the volume data, where $V$ is the number of voxels [S10, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Computationally tractable and suitable for interactive rendering, unlike full scattering models [S3, S10, unofficial][S17]. Performance is heavily dependent on the efficiency of volume traversal and interpolation [S8, unofficial].
